{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-matrices.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Matrices as moves of the plane",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Read a matrix as a move of the plane, work out from its columns where it sends any vector, and multiply matrices as one move after another.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Move the whole plane: stretch it, turn it, slant it, but keep the grid lines straight, parallel and evenly spaced, and keep $0$ where it is. Such a move is called **linear**. How much do you need to write down to know where every point goes?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -3.2, 3.8, -1.2, 3.8\nlet A = [2, -1; 1, 1]\nlet M = (1 - t)*[1, 0; 0, 1] + t*A\nG0 = grid([1, 0; 0, 1]) faint color 6\nG = grid(M) color 2\nI = arrow(0, M*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nLI = label(M*[1; 0], \"\\hat{\\imath}\") color 3\nJ = arrow(0, M*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nLJ = label(M*[0; 1], \"\\hat{\\jmath}\") color 4\nD1 = segment(M*[1; 0], M*[1; 1]) dashed color 4\nD2 = segment(M*[1; 1], M*[1; 2]) dashed color 4\nV = arrow(0, M*[1; 2]) color 1\nLV = label(M*[1; 2], \"v\") color 1\n> show D1, D2, V, LV | The grid, the unit arrows $\\hat{\\imath} = (1, 0)$ (green) and $\\hat{\\jmath} = (0, 1)$ (pink), and $v = (1, 2) = \\hat{\\imath} + 2\\hat{\\jmath}$: one step along $\\hat{\\imath}$, two along $\\hat{\\jmath}$.\n> play t to 1 in 4s | Move the plane. The grid lines stay straight, parallel and evenly spaced, and $0$ stays put. $\\hat{\\imath}$ lands on $(2, 1)$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ on $(-1, 1)$.\n> wait 1s | And $v$ is still one step along the new $\\hat{\\imath}$ and two along the new $\\hat{\\jmath}$: it lands on $(2, 1) + 2\\,(-1, 1) = (0, 3)$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Two arrows' worth. Every vector is $x\\,\\hat{\\imath} + y\\,\\hat{\\jmath}$, and a linear move keeps that recipe, so wherever $\\hat{\\imath}$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ land, $(x, y)$ lands on $x$ times the first landing spot plus $y$ times the second.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So write down the two landing spots, side by side as columns: that table is a **matrix**. Rows are separated by `;`, so the columns read downwards: here $\\hat{\\imath}$ lands on $(2, 1)$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ on $(-1, 1)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let A = [2, -1; 1, 1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Before the next cell: where does $A$ send $(1, 2)$, written as a column `[1; 2]`? Use only the columns.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A*[1; 2]",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same for any input $(x, y)$: $x$ times the first column plus $y$ times the second. That is the whole rule for a matrix times a vector, with nothing to memorise:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "x*[2; 1] + y*[-1; 1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A*[x; y]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Matrix times vector\n> $\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = x \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix} + y \\begin{pmatrix} b \\\\ d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} ax + by \\\\ cx + dy \\end{pmatrix}$. Each entry of the answer is a row of the matrix dotted with the vector.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[a, b; c, d]*[x; y]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Moves, read off their columns",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "To write down a move, ask where $\\hat{\\imath}$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ go. A quarter turn counterclockwise sends $\\hat{\\imath}$ to $(0, 1)$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ to $(-1, 0)$. A shear slides each point sideways by its height: $\\hat{\\imath}$ stays, and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ slides to $(1, 1)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let R = [0, -1; 1, 0]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let S = [1, 1; 0, 1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "R*[3; 1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "S*[3; 1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "One move after another",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Shear, then turn. Where do $\\hat{\\imath}$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ end up?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 2\nview -2.8, 2.8, -0.8, 2.6\nlet S = [1, 1; 0, 1]\nlet Sh = (1 - t)*[1, 0; 0, 1] + t*S\nlet Tn = [cos(pi*(t - 1)/2), -sin(pi*(t - 1)/2); sin(pi*(t - 1)/2), cos(pi*(t - 1)/2)]*S\nG0 = grid([1, 0; 0, 1]) faint color 6\nG1 = grid(Sh) color 2\nQ1 = poly(0, Sh*[1; 0], Sh*[1; 1], Sh*[0; 1]) color 1\nI1 = arrow(0, Sh*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nJ1 = arrow(0, Sh*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nG2 = grid(Tn) color 2\nQ2 = poly(0, Tn*[1; 0], Tn*[1; 1], Tn*[0; 1]) color 1\nI2 = arrow(0, Tn*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nJ2 = arrow(0, Tn*[0; 1]) thick color 4\n> show G1, Q1, I1, J1 | The unit square on $\\hat{\\imath}$ (green) and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ (pink).\n> play t to 1 in 2s | First the shear $S$: $\\hat{\\imath}$ stays put, $\\hat{\\jmath}$ slides over to $(1, 1)$.\n> hide G1, Q1, I1, J1; show G2, Q2, I2, J2 | Then the quarter turn $R$, applied to wherever things are now.\n> play t to 2 in 2s | $\\hat{\\imath}$ ends at $(0, 1)$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ at $(-1, 1)$: the columns of the combined move.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Each column of the combined move is $R$ applied to a column of $S$: the shear sent $\\hat{\\jmath}$ to $(1, 1)$, $S$'s second column, and the turn took it on from there. That is how matrices multiply. The combined move is written $RS$, with $S$ on the right, because it acts on a vector as $R(Sv)$: as in $f(g(x))$, the one written nearer the vector happens first.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "R*S",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "R*(S*[3; 1])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(R*S)*[3; 1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Matrix product\n> Column $j$ of $AB$ is $A$ times column $j$ of $B$: $AB$ is the move \"$B$, then $A$\". So its entry in row $i$, column $j$ is row $i$ of $A$ dotted with column $j$ of $B$, which is the quick way to compute it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The order matters",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Now turn first, then shear. Predict where $\\hat{\\imath}$ lands before the scene plays: the turn sends it to $(0, 1)$, and then the shear slides it sideways by its height.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 2\nview -2.8, 2.8, -0.8, 2.6\nlet R = [0, -1; 1, 0]\nlet S = [1, 1; 0, 1]\nlet Tn = [cos(pi*t/2), -sin(pi*t/2); sin(pi*t/2), cos(pi*t/2)]\nlet Sh = ((2 - t)*[1, 0; 0, 1] + (t - 1)*S)*R\nG0 = grid([1, 0; 0, 1]) faint color 6\nG1 = grid(Tn) color 2\nQ1 = poly(0, Tn*[1; 0], Tn*[1; 1], Tn*[0; 1]) color 1\nI1 = arrow(0, Tn*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nJ1 = arrow(0, Tn*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nG2 = grid(Sh) color 2\nQ2 = poly(0, Sh*[1; 0], Sh*[1; 1], Sh*[0; 1]) color 1\nI2 = arrow(0, Sh*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nJ2 = arrow(0, Sh*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nF = poly(0, [0, 1], [-1, 2], [-1, 1]) faint color 6\nFI = arrow(0, [0, 1]) faint color 3\nFJ = arrow(0, [-1, 1]) faint color 4\n> show G1, Q1, I1, J1 | The same square; this time the turn comes first.\n> play t to 1 in 2s | The quarter turn $R$: $\\hat{\\imath}$ goes to $(0, 1)$, $\\hat{\\jmath}$ to $(-1, 0)$.\n> hide G1, Q1, I1, J1; show G2, Q2, I2, J2 | Then the shear $S$, which slides each point sideways by its height.\n> play t to 2 in 2s | $\\hat{\\imath}$, at height $1$, slides to $(1, 1)$; $\\hat{\\jmath}$, at height $0$, stays at $(-1, 0)$.\n> show F, FI, FJ | Faint: where shear-then-turn left the square. A different move.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> With numbers the order of a product never matters, so the natural guess is $SR = RS$. The pictures already disagree, and so does the engine:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "S*R",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Some pairs do commute (two turns, for instance), but in general $AB \\neq BA$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[2, -1; 1, 1]*[3; -1]",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Where does $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ send $(3, -1)$? Work it out from the columns, then type it as a column, `[p; q]`.",
      "hints": [
        "$3$ times the first column minus the second: $3\\,(2, 1) - (-1, 1)$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[0, 1; 1, 0]",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Invent it: write the matrix of the mirror in the line $y = x$, which swaps $\\hat{\\imath}$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$.",
      "hints": [
        "Where does $\\hat{\\imath}$ land? That is the first column."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[0, -1; 1, 0]*[0, -1; 1, 0]",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "A quarter turn done twice is a half turn. Write its matrix from where $\\hat{\\imath}$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ land, then check that it is $RR$.",
      "hints": [
        "A half turn sends $\\hat{\\imath}$ to $(-1, 0)$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[1, 2; 3, 4]*[0, 1; 1, 0]",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Multiply. The right-hand matrix swaps $\\hat{\\imath}$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$, and it happens first: predict what that does to the columns of the left-hand one.",
      "hints": [
        "Column $1$ of the product is $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ times $(0, 1)$: its second column.",
        "The answer is a matrix: type it as `[p, q; r, s]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A matrix is a linear move of the plane, written down as where $\\hat{\\imath}$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ land, its columns. It sends $(x, y)$ to $x$ times the first column plus $y$ times the second. The product $AB$ is \"$B$, then $A$\", its columns $A$ times the columns of $B$, and the order matters.\n>\n> Lesson 2 ran the move forwards. Lesson 3 runs it backwards: given where a vector landed, which vector was it?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
